    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d681">

                <pb facs="0942" n="862" sameAs="frus:frus1936v01/pg862"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.114 Liquor/1159</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the South African Minister</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Close</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-08-15">August 15,
                        1936</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Minister</hi>: Since our pleasant
                    conversation on Wednesday last<anchor n="5" xml:id="d681-f1"/> I have undertaken
                    to inform myself more exactly regarding the period between promulgation and
                    incidence of regulations by the Federal Alcohol Control Administration. I have
                    been informed by responsible officers of the Administration as follows: <list>
                        <label>(1)</label>
                        <item>The Federal Alcohol Administration Act was passed August 29,
                                1935.<anchor n="6" xml:id="d681-f2"/> It provided that new
                            regulations both for wine and spirits would come into effect March 1,
                            1936. Public hearings were held in August 1935 and the wine regulations
                            were promulgated December 30, 1935, and those for spirits January 18,
                            1936.</item>
                        <label>(2)</label>
                        <item>The Administration found that it was a physical impossibility for
                            producers, even in this country, to make the necessary changes in the
                            six to eight weeks remaining before March 1. Accordingly it requested
                            the Congress to prolong the period and by Joint Resolution No. 217,
                            approved February 29, 1936,<anchor n="7" xml:id="d681-f3"/> the grace
                            periods were extended for spirits to August 15, 1936, and for wines to
                            December 15, 1936.</item>
                        <pb facs="0943" n="863" xml:id="pg863"/>
                        <label>(3)</label>
                        <item>Since the first issue of wine regulations December 30, 1935, there has
                            been no amendment whatsoever to these regulations, the only change
                            having been the effective date, which, of course, has been in favor of
                            producers everywhere. The spirits regulations have been amended because
                            of the complexities arising out of the bottled-in-bond section of the
                            act.</item>
                        <label>(4)</label>
                        <item>The Administration does not now perceive any cause for further
                            amendment of any of its regulations.</item>
                    </list>
                </p>
                <p>With regard to the importation of South African fruits I wish to assure you that
                    we are genuinely interested in arriving at an early solution of the present
                    difficulties and you may be sure that I shall do all that I can to cooperate
                    with other branches of the Government.</p>
                <p>Very sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">William Phillips</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
